# Beta Builds

Every push to a branch other than `main`, `gh-pages`, or a
`release-please--...` branch builds a **separate, installable beta app**. The
workflow can also be started manually with **Run workflow**. It publishes a
GitHub pre-release containing one APK, then removes older beta releases for
that branch. Deleting a branch removes its beta releases once `beta.yml` is on
the default branch.

Betas use the application ID `dev.co508.example.beta`, a distinct launcher
background colour, and a separate signing key, so they install beside the
production app. Each build uses the workflow run number as its `versionCode`;
its `versionName` is `<version.txt>-beta.<run>+<short sha>`. A new tag per
build lets update clients distinguish successive betas.

> TEMPLATE NOTE: replace `example` with the real app slug and
> `dev.co508.example` with the real application ID before using this file.

## Install

Download the latest APK for your branch from
<https://github.com/508-dev/example/releases> and open it on your phone. Beta
releases are marked **Pre-release** and their titles begin with `Beta`.

### Obtainium

1. Add the app from `https://github.com/508-dev/example`.
2. Turn on **Include prereleases**.
3. Set **Filter APKs by regular expression** to `example-beta`.
4. Set **Filter release titles by regular expression** to `^Beta`.

The APK filter prevents Obtainium from selecting the production APK attached to
a stable release. The title filter keeps it on beta pre-releases. Obtainium
will notify you when a new beta is published and can install it over the
previous beta while the signing key remains the same.

## Signing And Secrets

The repository secrets `BETA_KEYSTORE_BASE64`, `BETA_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD`,
`BETA_KEY_ALIAS`, and `BETA_KEY_PASSWORD` are generated by
`scripts/setup-beta-signing.sh` and belong only to the beta app; the production
signing key and its `Prod` environment are not used by this workflow. If the
beta key is lost, beta users must uninstall and reinstall the beta app rather
than update it in place.

On a fork, or if the keystore secret is unavailable, the workflow still runs
`./gradlew assembleBeta` as a compile check and skips publishing. Local beta
builds without signing environment variables use the debug key.
